      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the number of ways Johnny can choose three different marbles from his bag of seven different colored marbles, we can use the concept of combinations in combinatorics. \n\nEach marble has an equal chance of being chosen, and there is uncertainty as to which marble will be chosen first, second, or third. This type of probability is an example of a combination problem.\n\nSpecifically, it's a combination problem because we are interested in finding the number of distinct combinations of marbles without regard to the order in which they are chosen. The mathematical formula for a combination is:\n\n\\[C(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\]\n\nwhere \\(n\\) is the total number of objects to choose from, \\(k\\) is the number of objects to choose, and \\(!\\) denotes factorial, which means multiplying a series of descending natural numbers.\n\nFor our problem, we have \\(n = 7\\) (total marbles) and we need to choose \\(k = 3\\) marbles. Let's calculate the number of ways:\n\n\\[C(7, 3) = \\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} = \\frac{7!}{3!4!} = \\frac{7 \\times 6 \\times 5}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 35\\]\n\nSo Johnny can choose three different marbles from his bag of seven in 35 ways. The answer is written as:\n\n\\[\\boxed{35}\\]",
