      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Johnny has three ways to choose a first marble out of seven different sets.  He can then choose to pair each marble with a different second set of three, so there are $3*4$ ways to pair the marbles.  Then he can choose the last marble out of the remaining six, and again there are $3$ ways to do this.  Thus, there are \\[5*4*3 * 2 = 5*2*4*5*\\cancel{5}=30*40=1200\\]different ways to choose the three marbles.\n\nJohnny has ${7 \\choose 3} = 35$ ways to choose three different ones, and then he has ${7 \\choose 2} = 21$ ways to choose the fourth one. On the other hand, Johnny may pick the colors in different orders on the fifth and sixth marbles, so we must divide by $2$. Thus, the number of valid ways is  \\[\\frac {35 \\times 21}{2} = \\boxed{455}\\]",
